Publish notification history on the session bus, against v0.7.0.

Wayle stays the notification daemon: it owns `org.freedesktop.Notifications`,
holds the history, and is the only party that may invoke an action, because the
sending application is waiting on an `ActionInvoked` signal from the name it
talked to. What upstream does not do is publish enough of a notification for
anything else to draw one — `com.wayle.Notifications1.List` carries an id and
three strings, with no icon, image, actions, urgency or timestamp.

This adds `com.wayle.NotificationsExt1`, which carries the whole entry, plus
Invoke, Dismiss, DismissAll and ToggleDnd, and a Changed signal so a reader
redraws when something happens instead of polling. Every field it sends is
already on the in-memory model; nothing here changes how Wayle behaves.

Chrome marks its web notifications transient, which upstream shows as a popup
and then drops. They are kept in history here so the list has them after the
popup closes.

`scripts/control-centre` renders that list, so the notification card, the
calendar, the media players and the system readings are one panel.

